  "query_preview": "..\nMonday night through Wednesday: An upper-level low currently off the \nPNW coast around 130W is currently providing the Inland Northwest \nwith widespread middle and high level clouds as of Monday afternoon. \nThis low will dig southward off the California coast Monday night \ninto Tuesday morning, allowing subtle ridging to build into the \nInland Northwest. Temperatures trend warmer on Tuesday with less \ncloud cover. North winds will increase through the Okanogan Valley \nMonday night through Tuesday morning as high pressure builds into \nBritish Columbia and strengthens the CYLW-KPSC pressure gradient to \n+4-6 mb. This will support sustained winds 15-20 mph and gusts 25-30 \nmph down the Okanogan Valley Tuesday morning into the early \nafternoon. Winds will also increase, though not as strong, out of \nthe northeast through the Purcell Trench in north Idaho with \nsustained winds 12 to 18 mph. Temperatures on Wednesday will be \nsimilar to Tuesday, but with lighter winds as the north-south \npressure gradient decreases.\n\nThursday through Sunday: Changes in the weather pattern arrive \nThursday as the closed low over California begins to eject \nnortheastward and a frontal system with a trailing shortwave \napproaches from the west. While the frontal system looks mostly dry, \nmoisture and precipitation associated with the upper-level low \nmoving into far eastern Washington and north Idaho will bring a 30-\n50% chance of rain across far eastern Washington and a 50-80% chance \nacross north Idaho. Additionally, winds will be breezy to gusty from \nthe northwest to west with the cold front. With the KPDX-KGPI \nincreasing to +12-17 mb Friday morning,  there is high confidence \nfor wind gusts greater than 30 mph across central Washington and \nsoutheast Washington (60-80% chance) with a 30-50% chance across the \nSpokane area. Blowing dust is not anticipated to be a significant \nhazard given the lack of dust generation during recent comparable \nwind events.  However, this will mark the trend toward cooler \ndaytime high temperatures. \n\nBy the weekend, confidence in the weather pattern significantly \ndecreases regarding the strength and track of the next approaching \nweather system. Ensemble analysis reveals considerable variance: 42 \npercent of global ensemble members project a deeper trough \ninfluencing the Inland Northwest with cooler, wetter conditions. \nConversely, 25 percent maintain an offshore closed low, and 33 \npercent depict a flat ridge with a weaker system traversing northern \nBritish Columbia. Both of these latter scenarios favor a warmer, \ndrier sensible weather outcome. AI ensembles provide little \nconsensus, as the GEFS AI ensemble favors the cooler and wetter \nsolution (which is the opposite of the physics-based models) while \nthe ECMWF AIFS ensemble indicates only subtle mean troughing, \nhighlighting the uncertainty for the weekend. /vmt",
